<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kholodny Yar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kholodny Yar is a forested area of ravines near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chyhyryn">Chyhyryn</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/motronynskyi-monastery">Motronynskyi Monastery</a>. It is linked in Ukrainian memory with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate</a> and the haidamaka rising of 1768. From 1919 to 1922 local partisans there held a self-governing district against both White and Bolshevik forces, later celebrated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuri-horlis-horsky">Yuri Horlis-Horsky</a>'s memoir of the same name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kholodny-yar.jpg" alt="Kholodny Yar" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Uk-Kamelot · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9C%D0%BE%D1%82%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%BD%D1%81%D1%8C%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D0%BC%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B8%D1%801.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
